docs/architecture/MANIFESTO.md:21:Seventy-two systems. One hundred and five components. Three hundred and sixty-three items. A weather simulation, a lunar calendar, a town economy, a deity pantheon, a dungeon generator, particle VFX, procedural Perlin noise terrain, proc trees, a crafting bench, a hunger clock, a combat system with dual-wielding and affix modifiers — one hundred and thirty thousand lines of plain JavaScript, all of it deterministic, all of it in open air. No human could have typed this alone at this depth in this timeframe without either surrendering the constraints or surrendering the ambition.
docs/architecture/MANIFESTO.md:52:Because in a project like this, closure would not merely be a licensing choice. It would be a contradiction. JSHack's whole thesis is anti-enclosure. Anti-gate. Anti-priesthood. Anti-obscuring pipeline. To build a system around transparency, inspectability, directness, and hackability, and then retreat into concealment, would be to violate the work at the level of first principle.
